AB  - This paper sets out the creation of a dual convolutional neural network system that combines both one-dimensional and two-dimensional deep learning models. The 1D CNN takes individual pixel spectral data to build essential spectral features while the 2D CNN examines local area pixels to extract spatial information. The system obtains complete knowledge of hyperspectral data by combining the output signals from both neural network branches. The Pavia University benchmark dataset tests demonstrate that the hybrid 1D2D CNN architecture outperforms conventional machine learning approaches, including k-nearest neighbors (KNN), support vector machines (SVM), and Random Forest (RF), in terms of classification accuracy. Our method outperforms KNN by using both spectral and spatial information and surpasses SVM and RF because it employs two separate branches for spatial feature extraction. The fusion methodology leads to better classification precision and enhanced resistance to spectral noise as well as improved outcomes for class-overlapping situations. The results show that deep learning combined with spectral-spatial feature fusion effectively maps hyperspectral land cover.
