AB  - This paper proposes a quantitative model for identifying factors affecting landscape ecological service efficiency based on data mining, aiming to overcome the shortcomings of traditional methods. The model combines DNN with ensemble learning methods (random forest and XGBoost), and optimizes the hyperparameters of the model through PSO to improve its prediction accuracy. DNN is used to extract the multi-level characteristics of landscape ecological services, while ensemble learning algorithms are used to identify and quantify the weights of various influencing factors. In the experimental part, this paper selects three typical landscape areas, including forests, wetlands and urban green spaces, and combines remote sensing image data, environmental monitoring data and climate data for model training and verification. The results show that the model proposed in this paper can significantly improve the prediction accuracy in different landscape areas, especially in forest and wetland areas, where the model performs better than the traditional regression analysis method. The simulation results show that the RMSE in the forest area is 0.29, and the MAE in the wetland area is 0.21, which is about 15%-20% higher than the traditional method. Through the weight analysis of the influencing factors output by the model, it is found that climate change, vegetation coverage and land use are the key influencing factors of landscape ecological service efficiency. Generally speaking, this model can accurately identify and quantify the major factors affecting the efficiency of eco-service, and provide reliable data for ecological management and decision making.
