AB  - Automatic road extraction has been an active research problem for the past few decades, with Deep Learning (DL)-based methods representing the state of the art. While most existing approaches rely on satellite imagery, Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) have recently provided new insights into this topic. The high performance of DL-based models is typically achieved when large amounts of annotated road data are available and when shifts in data distributions are minimal. In particular, domain shifts between training and testing sets can significantly hinder the generalization ability of these models during inference. To address this challenge while reducing road labeling efforts, we propose an approach based on Unsupervised Domain Adaptation (UDA), which leverages the Domain Adversarial Neural Network (DANN) method. Specifically, we adapt DANN for semantic segmentation using the U-Net architecture with attention blocks, focusing exclusively on UAV data. From the obtained results, UDA improves the road extraction performance over the baseline by 5% in terms of F1-Score and Intersection over Union metrics. When incorporating the attention mechanism, this strategy further refines the UDA approach with an enhancement of approximately 1.05% for both metrics.
ER  -

