AB  - In the complex tea garden environments, accurately detecting diseased tea leaves and promptly implementing control measures are crucial to preventing the further spread of tea plant diseases. Addressing challenges such as significant morphological variations in tea leaf blight spots and high similarity between spots and backgrounds in UAV-acquired images, this paper proposes a tea leaf blight detection network TLBDNet for UAV remote sensing images. This network is based on YOLOv11 and designs a multi-aspect information extraction component (MIEC) to enhance the model's feature discrimination ability for disease spots. An adaptive feature aggregation (AFA) module is constructed to integrate global contextual information and suppress false positives caused by complex backgrounds such as camellias and soil. At the same time, an adaptive threshold focus loss (ATFL) function is introduced as a confidence loss function to accelerate the convergence speed of the model. To verify the effectiveness of the designed network for detecting tea leaf blight in UAV remote sensing images, this paper conducted experiments on a self-built tea leaf blight dataset. The results showed that the detection accuracy of TLBDNet reached 80.3%, with an average precision (mAP) of 76.3%. Compared to existing detection networks, TLBDNet achieves the best detection performance for tea leaf blight in UAV remote sensing images.
