AB  - The temporal and spatial effects of urbanization, industrialization and agricultural expansions on the ecosystems of the regions can only be understood by monitoring Land Use and Land Cover (LULC) changes. This paper examines the three decades of LULC changes between 1994 and 2024 in Tamil nadu, India, based on the multi-temporal Landsat imagery and machine learning on the Google Earth engine platform. Cloud masking, atmospheric correction and clipping to state boundary were done in order to make Landsat 5, 7 and 8 datasets consistent. Two supervised algorithms Support Vector Machine (SVM) and Random Forest (RF) were used to create the classified LULC maps that identified the six categories of the land surface which included Water, Forest, Built-up, Agriculture, Fallow Land and Mines. Comparative accuracy Testing showed that the RF classifier had a greater overall accuracy and kappa value than that of SVM especially in spectrally mixed areas. As indicated by the time analysis, the build-up and fallow land areas have increased significantly with a sharp reduction in the forest and agricultural lands. These results indicate the high rate of urbanization and resource overexploitation in Tamil Nadu with the necessity to apply sustainable land use and environmental planning based on evidence-based data.
