AB  - Coastal aquaculture ponds play a vital role in the marine fisheries economy by providing a sustainable source of seafood. Remote sensing methods are currently used to extract coastal aquaculture pond information based on machine learning. However, due to the impact of spatial heterogeneity and the relatively weak robustness of single machine learning models, these methods show poor performance in large-scale coastal aquaculture pond remote sensing mapping. To bridge this methodological gap, the study developed a comprehensive framework for large-scale extraction of coastal aquaculture ponds, leveraging multi-sensor satellite observations (Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 MSI) within a multi-stage analytical pipeline that hybridizes hierarchical decision tree with ensemble learning algorithms. This framework was implemented using the Google Earth Engine (GEE) cloud platform for large-scale, automated, and high-precision remote sensing mapping of coastal aquaculture ponds. First, image preprocessing and feature stacking were employed to obtain high-quality imagery data. Hexagonal tiles were subsequently used to partition the study area, overcoming the limitations of the study area size and cloud platform computational resources. Then, extraction of coastal aquaculture ponds from remote sensing imagery was carried out using a combined hierarchical decision tree and ensemble learning approach. A hierarchical decision tree with multi-feature support was used for the coarse extraction of coastal aquaculture ponds, overcoming the limitations of a single remote sensing data source in expressing coastal aquaculture pond features. An ensemble learning model integrating spatial neighborhood information was subsequently used for the fine extraction of coastal aquaculture ponds, fully utilizing the complementary advantages of multiple classifiers to address spatial heterogeneity issues and improve classification performance. The model constructed in this work was successfully applied to the remote sensing mapping of coastal aquaculture ponds in Zhejiang Province, China. The average overall accuracy of the pond extraction results improved by 1.07%–10.09% compared to random forest (RF), classification, regression trees (CART) and support vector machine (SVM), reaching an overall accuracy of 87.90%. This approach yielded a Kappa coefficient and F1 score of 75.88% and 88.16%, respectively. The study exhibited robust performance in geographically complex coastal zone, providing dependable technical support for integrated coastal management and sustainable fisheries development.
