AB  - River fragmentation, caused by the construction of dam-related barriers such as dams, locks, and weirs, is a major issue impacting aquatic ecosystems and water quality. This study combined deep learning, remote sensing imagery, and manual validation to detect dam-related barriers in major river basins of China and focused on the river fragmentation levels and the impacts of these barriers on water quality. The proposed deep learning model achieved high performance with a precision of 0.9112 and a recall of 0.8940 in barrier detection, and generated a dam-related barrier dataset comprising 12,962 instances. Results show that low-head dams (55 %) and partial dams (26 %) are the most prevalent dam-related barrier types. Connectivity metrics revealed that the Southeast Basin exhibited the highest fragmentation, while the Southwest Basin exhibited the lowest. Only 24.04 % of the river network remained connected under all-barrier conditions, compared to 40.87 % when only large dams were considered, indicating more severe fragmentation primarily driven by the widespread distribution of small dams. Geographically weighted regression model analysis showed stable performance across scenarios (PBIAS − 7 to + 3) and revealed that the cumulative impact of small dams on conductivity, turbidity, permanganate index, and total nitrogen often exceeded that of individual large dams. These findings demonstrate the critical role of small dams in altering water quality and river connectivity, and highlight the need to consider their cumulative effects in river management strategies.
