AB  - This research investigates the geographical and temporal generalizability of machine learning models trained to classify multispectral (MS) data (ASTER) using hyperspectral (HS)-derived mineral maps as ground truth. An extensive cross-validation was performed across multiple sites in Nevada, USA, and Pilbara, Western Australia, to systematically evaluate inter-regional and inter-temporal transferability of the models. As a result, the predictive performance of the models degrades substantially when applied to different geographical regions or to the same region at a different time, while models achieve high accuracy in self-validation. This generalization failure persists even when simplifying the problem to a binary classification (e.g., kaolinite vs. other minerals) and, critically, when using MS data from the same acquisition pass over different sites, indicating that observation geometry is not the primary cause. Consequently, we concluded that the current models are suitable only for site-specific spectral patterns rather than capturing universal mineral features. This is due to a fundamental "reality mismatch" between the idealized, pure-pixel HS training labels and the inherently spectrally-mixed-pixel nature of the MS data. These findings highlight a critical limitation to the direct transferability of such models, suggesting that their effective application may be limited to target areas that share highly similar spectral mixture patterns with the training site. Future work should address this mismatch to create more widely applicable mineral mapping tools.
