AB  - Hyperspectral satellite remote sensing imagery is a state-of-the-art technique to obtain important information on the presence, distribution, and composition of crystalline weathering products and alteration minerals in extreme environments such as Antarctica. The Dry valleys (DVs) of South Victoria Land in Antarctica, one of the most extreme deserts on Earth, characterized by a cold, dry climate and minimal atmospheric moisture, provide a superlative analog to the landscapes of Mars and an important record of Earth's geological history; although they occupy only a tiny fraction of the continent's area, the DVs comprise approximately 25 % of all snow-free exposed lithology outcrops across Antarctica. In this study, hyperspectral PRISMA satellite images were meticulously analyzed using 3-dimensional Convolutional Neural Networks (3-D CNNs) to generate a regional mineral map for the DVs. To achieve accurate classification and mapping of minerals in the DVs, an innovative methodology combining Adaptive Vertex Component Analysis (AVCA), spectral augmentation, and 3-D CNNs was developed. Numerous endmember spectra extracted from the PRISMA datasets show a variety of important absorption and reflectance features associated with different mineral groups, such as biotite, calcite, corrensite, epidote, chlorite, jarosite, sericite, kaolinite/smectite, limonite/goethite/hematite, mirabilite/thenardite, montmorillonite/illite, nontronite, plagioclase, pyroxenes, vermiculite and zeolite. Mineral maps were then produced for several selected exposed zones and compared to lithological maps generated using the GeoMAP dataset (v.2022-08) of the DVs. The evaluation of the performance of the deep learning algorithm shows that 3-D CNNs exhibit high training and validation accuracy, with values between 0.994 and 0.997, besides training and validation loss values between 0.10 and 0.11, reflecting a robust and reliable performance. Consequently, a regional mineral map for the DVs was generated using the 3-D CNNs algorithm on hyperspectral PRISMA satellite imagery, accurately. This study demonstrates the great potential of hyperspectral PRISMA satellite imagery and deep learning techniques for mineralogical studies in extreme environments and provides valuable insights into the geological evolution of Antarctica and similar extraterrestrial terrains.
