AB  - Hyperspectral imaging (HSI) has been recognized as an advanced remote sensing technique owing to its capability to acquire highly specific spectral information using hundreds of adjacent bands, thereby leading to accurate identification of land cover, vegetation, minerals, water resources, and urban areas. However, the problem with the hyperspectral data is the difficulty associated with the high dimensionality and complexity of data, which poses a great challenge to analyzing and classifying them. Thanks to the developments in the field of artificial intelligence (AI) over the last few decades, there has been a huge improvement in the accuracy of hyperspectral images' classification tasks. Initial studies involved the use of techniques such as dimensionality reduction and conventional machine learning methods like SVMs and RFs to solve the problem of high dimensionality. The development of deep learning gave rise to CNN, RNN, GNN, and Transformer-based models that learn discriminative spectral-spatial representations in an automatic way. Self-supervised learning, multimodal learning, vision-language model, and XAI have been used recently to improve the accuracy of classification, robustness, and explainability. study highlights the transition from conventional machine learning to explainable and multimodal intelligence with the discussion of existing problems and future perspectives of reliable hyperspectral remote sensing systems.
