AB  - Accurate monitoring of coastal wetlands is crucial for supporting effective conservation. Integrating rich temporal, spatial, and spectral features from multitemporal multisource images for coastal wetland monitoring has become research hotspot and challenging task in the field of remote sensing. However, given heterogeneity and characteristics of diverse modalities, it is challenge to integrate multisource data, especially interaction and fusion of them. Moreover, current studies tend to be incapable of extract both local spatiotemporal features reflecting short-term fluctuations and global spatiotemporal features associated with long-term phenological dependencies. This letter proposes a novel multidimensional feature extraction and cross-modal fusion network (STCMI). We first extract global features for phenology and local spatiotemporal for fluctuations from multitemporal data in stages. The feature interaction module (FIM) is designed with multiscale asymmetric spatial convolution and spectral pooling, enabling deep cross-modal interaction and focusing on complementary information from modalities. The cross-modal fusion module (CFM) uses complementary information extracted from FIM to effectively guide attention fusion. The result shows our approach outperforms other methods, validating its superiority and effectiveness. Further Ablation experiments validate the effect of multisource and multitemporal data in classification.
