AB  - Methane is an important greenhouse gas and the rapid detection of it is essential for mitigating climate change and ensuring safety for the environment. Conventional approaches for detecting methane typically do not provide the accuracy or scale needed while flying through complicated terrain with aerial or hyperspectral images. By proposing a hybrid deep-learning approach that combines the spatial segmentation of U-Net and the semantic segmentation of DeepLabV3+, this work seeks to overcome some of the limitations of traditional approaches by accurately segmenting methane plumes using hyperspectral data. The proposed system was trained with hyperspectral data at a resolution eligible for NASA EMIT (Earth Surface Mineral Dust Source Investigation Project) and included appropriate preprocessing steps such as radiometric correction and spectral enhancement. In differentiating methane plumes from background noise, cloud obscuration and spectral mixing, it is critical utilize these techniques so the deep learning model can create adequate extraction boundaries for accurate results. Our hybrid model obtained an accuracy of 88% respectively. Importantly, this framework will be able to be independent and scalable meaning it can be used with other satellites or aerial platforms and to help facilitate the mechanization of the monitoring system in real-time. This work illustrates how a hybrid, relatable deep learning models using domain-specific information could effectively improve global monitoring of the environment, and act quickly, accordingly.
