AB  - In recent years, Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Graph Convolutional Networks (GCNs) have been employed extensively in hyperspectral image classification. However, CNN-based methods face limitations due to their fixed convolutional kernels, which hinder the effective extraction of features from hyperspectral data in non-Euclidean space. Moreover, the limited receptive field of convolutional kernels in CNNs limits their capacity to fully capture global spatial-spectral features of Hyperspectral Images (HSIs). Nevertheless, GCN-based methods encounter challenges in constructing graph structures when dealing with large numbers of hyperspectral pixels. Traditional GCNs also struggle to model spatial relationships among distant nodes directly, which limits their ability to capture long-range interactions within the input data effectively. To address these challenges, this paper proposes an innovative Non-Euclidean Spectral-Spatial Feature Mining Network with Gated GCN-CNN (NESSGGCN) for hyperspectral image classification. The proposed network employs a weighted fusion of Gated Graph Convolutional Network architecture to extract spectral-spatial features from non-Euclidean space of HSIs, alongside pixel-level convolutional neural networks for capturing spectral-spatial features from Euclidean space. This approach simultaneously compensates for the inherent limitation of graph convolution by addressing the failure to account for long-range dependencies between distant nodes. Experiments conducted on the WHU-Hi-HongHu, WHU-Hi-HanChuan and YRD-GF-5 datasets demonstrate that the proposed method achieves overall classification accuracies of 98.48%, 98.33% and 94.22%, respectively. These results represent improvements of 1.43%, 2.16% and 1.28% compared to the current state-of-the-art classification methods. And the proposed method exhibits strong robustness and generalizability in hyperspectral image classification tasks. The source code is released at https://github.com/KustAIRS/NESSGGCN.
