AB  - Hyperspectral remote imaging (HSI) in urban areas has recently garnered increasing attention from the scientific community. Key drivers are the successful launch and data distribution of a new generation of hyperspectral satellites, i.e. PRISMA and EnMAP missions. Enabling the capture of detailed spectral information, HSI already proves useful for applications in mineral exploration, vegetation analysis, etc. Its applicability to urban environments may also be beneficial, as HS can discriminate artificial materials that, based on multispectral data, often exhibit very similar spectral responses. However, some challenges arise, such as the mixture of land covers at small scales and the geometric complexity of urban areas, which lead to high intraclass variability and difficulties in sample collection. These constraints highlight the need for a systematic assessment of the current state-of-the-art regarding spaceborne sensors. To conduct this analysis, an evidence base was gathered from scientific literature, excluding grey literature, namely anything not peer reviewed. Scopus was used to gather 1,081 peer-reviewed articles, reduced to 113 through a screening process. Key insights were identified, such as the continued prevalence of Machine Learning algorithms for analysis and the limited integration of multi-sensor and multi-source datasets. Among the many possible applications of hyperspectral remote sensing in urban contexts, this work found that a novel testing ground for second generation satellite products is rising, i.e. classification and analysis of urban vegetation. PRISMA and EnMAP’s spectral, spatial and temporal resolution open new possibilities to multi-temporal analysis of urban vegetation patterns, allowing investigating and developing novel approaches for the measurement and monitoring of urban contexts. In this regard, the paper will present a methodological concept and the initial outcome of a classification of urban vegetation based on HSI data over the urban test site of Rome, a European capital city characterized by wide and distributed parks and urban green spaces.
