AB  - Phase retrieval is a critical technique for reconstructing optical field information. By reconstructing the phase of the light field from intensity measurements, this approach effectively address phase distortions induced by atmospheric turbulence and optical system imperfections, substantially enhancing the quality and multidimensional information content of remote sensing imagery, thereby providing critical support for disaster monitoring, resource exploration, and climate research. In recent years, the rapid development of deep learning has offered unprecedented support for computational optical imaging. However, traditional deep learning techniques in phase retrieval often rely on large sample datasets and deep network models, which pose numerous challenges in practical applications. To address this issue, this paper proposes a novel lightweight network, StarNet. Results of numerical simulations demonstrate that StarNet exhibits superior performance in handling higher-order modes and arbitrary intensity optical fields under random phase perturbations. It can effectively extract phase distortion information under few-shot conditions and accurately recover the intensity of the optical field. This few-shot phase-retrieval algorithm not only paves the way for practical advancements in computational imaging but also offers new approaches into data-scarce computational problems, such as remote sensing imaging and signal processing.
