AB  - As a dominant component of the Three-North Shelter Forest Program (TNSFP) in the Three-North Region of China (TNR), farmland shelterbelts (FSs or windbreaks) play crucial roles in reducing wind erosion and soil degradation. However, large-scale FS products are currently unavailable, posing challenges for their monitoring and optimization. To address this issue, we developed a deep learning-based framework to extract FSs from Sentinel-2 data and analyze their spatial patterns in the TNR. This study produced the first 10-m resolution FS map and the first vectorized FS network for the TNR in 2022. Our results indicated that the total length of FSs was 915.09 ± 21.45 thousand km, while the total area was 1575.18 ± 58.75 thousand ha. Both FS length and area were highest in Heilongjiang, Xinjiang, and Inner Mongolia, whereas the lowest values were observed in Beijing, Tianjin, and Qinghai. The average FS to farmland ratio, connectivity, and FS deviation angle were 2.83 % (± 0.11 %), 0.34, and 26°, respectively, highlighting potential for structural optimization. FSs were more concentrated, with well-structured spatial patterns in Northeast China and Xinjiang, while further construction efforts were needed in the Hetao Plain, Guanzhong Plain, and Hexi Corridor. The final FS map demonstrated robust performance across multiple evaluation metrics. Specifically, the pixel-based user's accuracy was 79.20 % and the producer's accuracy was 79.39 %. The average object-based F1-score was 84.13 % and the average length-weighted F1-score was 89.62 %. Overall, this study provides fundamental data and valuable insights to support agricultural infrastructure assessment and optimization.
