AB  - Hyperspectral image classification has been widely applied in ecological protection scenarios, such as forest monitoring and tree species investigation. However, hyperspectral classification still faces problems of high intra-class variability and inter-class similarity. Feature selection and spatial-spectral feature extraction are mostly adopted to address these problems. In this paper, we propose a novel lightweight Spectral-Spatial 3D convolutional architecture (3D-LiSS) with data processing and contextual attention for hyperspectral classification. Specifically, we first designed the patch and projection processing for feature selection to alleviate the numerous 3D convolutional parameters and the large computation. Then, the presented 3D-Conv module extracts and aggregates spatial-spectral features by a three-layer 3D convolutional neural network with different receptive field kernels. Finally, a novel 2D-Conv is built to extract contextual information of spatial dimension representation for efficient classification. Experimental results on SZUTreeHSI-R1 sufficiently demonstrate that the 3D-LiSS has excellent classification accuracy across all 17 tree species categories, exhibiting an outstanding capability for spatial-spectral feature extraction for hyperspectral image classification.
