AB  - Traditional evaluations of mine restoration benefits often rely on qualitative methods, which makes it difficult to achieve quantitative and dynamic comparisons across projects. To address this limitation, this paper proposes an ecological benefit calculation model for mine restoration that integrates deep learning with Data Envelopment Analysis (DEA). First, deep learning techniques are employed to automatically extract and predict ecological indicators from multi-source data such as remote sensing imagery, soil monitoring records, and biodiversity surveys. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) are applied to obtain refined vegetation coverage indices, while recurrent neural networks (RNNs) are used to capture temporal variations in soil physicochemical properties and ecosystem service values. These outputs, together with traditional inputs (funding, labor, land reclamation area, and technical equipment), form a high-dimensional input-output dataset. Next, CCR and BCC DEA models are introduced to compute the relative efficiency of each restoration unit, identify input redundancies and output shortfalls, and provide targeted improvement directions. An empirical study of the Hashotan open-pit coal mine restoration project in Inner Mongolia demonstrates that the deep learning-enhanced DEA model achieves more accurate and discriminative efficiency evaluations compared to traditional DEA, while effectively handling data gaps and uncertainties. The results highlight that deep learning significantly improves the quality of ecological indicators, thereby strengthening DEA's ability to differentiate restoration performance. Finally, sensitivity and robustness analyses confirm the model's stability. This study offers a novel methodological framework that combines artificial intelligence with efficiency analysis, providing scientific evidence and practical references for refined mine restoration management and ecological decision-making.
