AB  - Spatiotemporal fusion of multisource data offers a promising solution for high-resolution and temporally dense observations. However, most existing fusion methods struggle with cloud-contaminated auxiliary image scenarios, substantially limiting their applicability. While synthetic aperture radar (SAR) provides a viable solution, current fusion frameworks are largely tailored to optical imagery. To bridge this gap, this study proposes an information-progressive Optical-SAR spatiotemporal fusion network (IOS-Net) that achieves deep cross-modal fusion of Landsat-8, MODIS, and Sentinel-1 data via hierarchical progressive integration. The architecture implements iterative feature extraction, information injection, filtering, and fusion across multilevel representations, effectively reconstructing high spatiotemporal resolution cloud-free imagery. IOS-Net was evaluated in 13 experiments across 2 highly dynamic and frequently cloud-covered areas. Results consistently demonstrate superior performance over four representative deep learning methods, particularly for rapid surface changes and heavy cloud contamination, yielding average spatial-spectral accuracy improvements of 9.8% and 9.4% over the best baseline method in the two study areas, respectively. Experimental analysis confirms that its progressive information injection strategy effectively leverages the complementary strengths of multisource data, while Sentinel-1 integration significantly improves subcloud reconstruction fidelity. This exploratory study advances optical-SAR spatiotemporal fusion research and exhibits practical value for spatiotemporal information reconstruction in cloud-contaminated scenarios.
