AB  - Hyperspectral image (HSI) classification is critical for precision agriculture and analogous applications, yet it is constrained by inadequate deep feature mining, insufficient cross-dimensional feature interaction and unsatisfactory feature fusion performance. This paper proposes an MDRA-MKL HSI classification method integrating multidimensional dense residual attention networks and multi-kernel learning. The framework builds three homogeneous network branches equipped with 1D, 2D, and 3D convolutions to extract hierarchical spectral, spatial, and joint spatial-spectral deep features. Combined with dense connections and adaptive residual attention modules, the method facilitates efficient feature reuse and strengthens representative feature learning. On this basis, a centered kernel alignment (CKA)-based multi-kernel learning strategy adaptively projects the three types of deep features into high-dimensional spaces and implements weighted fusion to construct discriminative composite kernels, followed by support vector machine-based classification. Evaluated on Indian Pines and Longkou datasets, the proposed method achieves superior overall accuracy, average accuracy and Kappa coefficient compared with baseline approaches. It effectively enhances small-sample HSI classification performance and verifies the efficacy of the integrated multidimensional feature fusion and multi-kernel learning mechanism.
