AB  - Cloud water, as a key component of clouds, plays a critical role in cloud-related processes such as precipitation and radiation. However, some early satellite observation datasets exhibit some inaccuracies in distinguishing cloud water phases. This paper aims to obtain more accurate cloud water data for earlier years. To achieve this, machine learning schemes are developed to predict the global monthly mean ice water fraction (IWF). These predictions are based on CloudSat-CALIPSO satellite observations, International Satellite Cloud Climatology Project (ISCCP) dataset and the fifth-generation Earth Reanalysis Atmospheric (ERA5) reanalysis data. Four machine learning algorithms are used: Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LGB), eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Random Forest (RF) and Multilayer Perceptron (MLP). The results show that all four schemes achieve high predictive accuracy on the test set. However, LGB outperforms in computational efficiency, and is selected for the SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) analysis and final long-term reconstruction. A new metric is defined by integrating coefficient of determination (R2) and training time to comprehensively evaluate the performance of machine learning schemes called R2-Time score (RTS). SHAP analysis indicates that specific humidity, latitude, and relative humidity are the main factors influencing IWF prediction, and the LGB scheme's prediction mechanism aligns with physical expectation. The prediction mechanisms of the scheme do not exhibit strong temporal or spatial dependency. Ultimately, the study uses the LGB scheme to reconstruct global monthly mean IWF data from July 1983 to December 2009. The reconstructed IWF dataset successfully captures distinct annual cycles and reproduces the climatic variability observed in ISCCP and CloudSat-CALIPSO records, while simultaneously correcting the systematic biases in cloud-phase classification present in ISCCP. This study offers both improved historical datasets and methodological advances for investigations of cloud–climate interactions. Plain language summary Clouds have an important impact on radiation, precipitation, and the water cycle in the Earth-atmosphere system. As a key component of clouds, cloud water plays a decisive role in cloud formation and dissipation, precipitation, and radiation processes. However, early passive remote sensing satellite observations have errors in distinguishing cloud water phases. To obtain more accurate cloud water phase data, this study trains four machine learning schemes using multi-source data from CloudSat-CALIPSO, International Satellite Cloud Climatology Project (ISCCP) and the fifth-generation Earth Reanalysis Atmospheric (ERA5) reanalysis data to predict ice water fraction (IWF): Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LGB), eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Random Forest (RF) and Multilayer Perceptron (MLP). We propose a new metric, R2-Time score (RTS), to comprehensively evaluate the predictive accuracy and computational efficiency of machine learning schemes. Additionally, we verify the generalization ability of the machine learning schemes and evaluate the interpretability of the LGB scheme using SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) method. Finally, we reconstruct the global monthly mean IWF data from July 1983 to December 2009 using the LGB scheme, providing both data support and methodological reference for future cloud-climate feedback analysis and historical climate studies.
