AB  - Graph convolutional networks (GCNs) have recently shown great promise in hyperspectral image classification (HIC) by effectively modeling long-range correlations among samples. However, existing methods often rely on sample similarity for graph construction while neglecting intra-class variability, thereby limiting the robustness of the extracted features. This article introduces a novel HIC method that leverages sample inherent correlations to enhance feature representation and improve classification performance. Specifically, for the labeled sample set, two distinct intra-class sample graphs are constructed: the intra-class sample similarity graph (ISSG) for capturing class cohesiveness, and the intra-class sample discrepancy graph (ISDG) for modeling variability within the same class. These complementary graph structures are integrated into a dual-branch GCN model, where the ISSG-based branch acts as the primary classifier and the ISDG-based branch serves as an auxiliary component. During training, contrastive learning is employed to enhance feature alignment and generalization. For testing, category prediction is performed on the primary branch using a graph structure constructed from an augmented sample set, comprising training samples, test samples, and the spatial neighbors of the test samples within a predefined local window based on similarity metrics. Finally, the class label for each test sample is determined as the dominant semantic category within its local spatial window. Experimental results on four public hyperspectral image (HIS) datasets demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms state-of-the-art techniques and existing graph-based approaches.
