AB  - Mining-induced ground cracks pose threats to ecological security, infrastructure stability, and mine safety. Automated segmentation from unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) images in mining areas faces typical challenges: severe feature dilution of narrow and tiny cracks, strong interference from uneven illumination and rock textures, as well as the challenging tradeoff between accuracy and efficiency. To target these field-specific problems, this letter proposes CrackNet-MR, a U-Net-based customized segmentation model with dual-module synergy for high-precision and lightweight mining crack extraction. The model equips two collaborative tailored modules: the mine crack resilient feature block (MCRF-Block) and the multidimensional cooperative dual-cross attention (MCDCA) module, which are specially designed to enhance tiny crack feature retention and suppress complex background interference. Experiments on the mining area dataset show that CrackNet-MR achieves 80.54% mIoU with leading precision, recall, and F1-score. With only 6.21M parameters and 8.59 frames/s, the model strikes a favorable balance between segmentation accuracy and computational efficiency, providing a practical and lightweight solution for intelligent geological hazard monitoring in mining areas.
