AB  - The precise detection of mining activities using deep learning (DL) on satellite imagery has been paving innovative paths for environmental monitoring and preservation, regulatory compliance, and natural resource management in the mining industry. Nevertheless, existing DL based models confront some constraints: 1) Critical implementation aspects are not specified, which limits reproducibility. 2) Most were evaluated on a single train-validation split, which may not fully reflect model generalization. 3) Benchmarking was done only against the baseline model and are implemented on slightly outdated baseline DL models, with the highest AP@50 reaching just 0.575 on a publicly available mining detection dataset. To address these limitations, this study proposes an enhanced DL model, based on the YOLOv11 architecture, for surface mining detection. The proposed architecture incorporates four channel attention modules to strengthen feature representation, three content-aware reassembly of features (CARAFE) upsampling modules to replace the standard upsampling operations to enable content-aware feature upscaling, and an extra detection head to enhance multiscale detection. The model is assessed on a new Canada-wide mining detection dataset. Compared to baseline YOLOv11, the proposed model demonstrates a 0.9% increment in AP@50, while outperforming MFTA-YOLO, which is previously identified as the best performing model for mining detection domain, by 2.9% in AP@50. Furthermore, it has consistent improvements across multiple different splits, indicating reliable generalization capability. Visual review of predicted outputs confirms more accurate detection, while Grad-CAM visualizations further validate that the proposed model outperforms the existing state-of-the-art models.
