AB  - This study aims to enhance the accuracy of identifying Martian hydrated minerals, thereby advancing the exploration of Martian geology and the search for potential signs of life. Investigating Martian hydrated minerals not only helps to reconstruct the planet's water history but also provides important clues in the search for possible life on Mars. To overcome challenges such as complex spectral features, limited data availability, and noise interference, this work proposes a hybrid model combining Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Random Forests (RFs) for multi-class classification. First, CNN is employed to extract deep spectral features from Martian hyperspectral imagery. Unlike traditional approaches, CNNs leverage their multi-layer architectures and strong feature-learning capabilities to capture subtle spectral differences, addressing the limitations of earlier methods in processing complex spectral characteristics. Subsequently, RF is applied to classify the features extracted by CNN. By integrating multiple decision trees via a voting mechanism, RF leverages its robustness to small sample sizes and noisy data, thereby improving the model's stability and generalization. To mitigate the issue of limited sample size in the MICA spectral library, data augmentation techniques are adopted, introducing small random perturbations and noise to the spectral curves to enhance the model's adaptability to real Martian data. Based on labeled training samples and an independent test dataset, the experimental results indicate that the CNN-RF hybrid model significantly outperforms conventional models such as CNN, RF, Artificial Neural Networks (ANN), and Support Vector Machines (SVC), as well as the deep convolutional model MICAnet, in key performance metrics including the F1 score, precision, and recall. Notably, the model achieves 99.9% accuracy in identifying iceminerals. Moreover, it demonstrates substantial improvements in several mineral groups, such as hydrated silicates and halides, with recognition efficiencies increased by 25.89% and 22.65%, respectively, compared with CNN and RF alone. These results confirm the advantages of the CNN-RF hybrid model in processing complex spectral data, providing an efficient and accurate solution for Martian mineral spectral identification. Future research will focus on integrating multi-source data, refining model parameters, and enhancing model transparency and interpretability to improve performance and applicability further. This work offers valuable data and effective methodologies for future Mars sample-return missions.
ER  -

