AB  - Aiming at the severe challenges to the accuracy and robustness of the detection model caused by the complexity problems, such as high object density, dramatic scale changes, occlusion, variable illumination and class imbalance in remote sensing image object detection, this study innovatively proposes a multiscale adaptive remote sensing network (MARS-Net). First, the spatial context network (SCNet) is constructed. The adaptive feature extraction of complex object morphology is realized by the conditional convolution module, which significantly improves the adaptability of the model to diverse objects in remote sensing images. At the same time, SCNet integrates the shift window transformer module to effectively capture the long-distance dependencies between objects and enhance the model's semantic understanding of complex scenes. In addition, an enhanced multiscale feature pyramid network (EMFPN) is designed. The core of EMFPN is the global-offset and detail aggregation module, which integrates the low-stage feature alignment module and the high-stage feature alignment module of the gold module. The simple aggregation module is used to efficiently fuse multiscale information from different feature layers, thereby improving the feature utilization efficiency. The low-stage information fusion module and the high-stage information fusion module use omni-dimensional dynamic convolution to more comprehensively model the spatial information of the object and accurately process the direction and deformation of the object. The high-stage information fusion module also incorporates the exponential moving average attention mechanism to dynamically adjust the attention weight of features, further enhancing the discriminative ability of key features and the robustness of the overall model. The experimental results show that MARS-Net improves mAP@50 by 3.38% and 1.21% on RSOD and DOTAv2.0 datasets, and mAP@50:95 by 14.61% and 0.55%, respectively, which fully verifies its superior performance and practical application potential in complex remote sensing scenarios.
