AB  - Traditional coal preparation processes in mines suffer from low gangue sorting accuracy and efficiency, while ore element content determination relies on chemical analysis, which is time-consuming, labor-intensive, and costly. This paper explores the advantages of spatial-spectral joint feature extraction using convolutional neural networks and the principles of salient object detection, optimizing model design for mining applications. In coal preparation tasks, the CNN model achieves 92%-96% accuracy in detecting coal particles, with both precision and recall exceeding 90%, significantly exceeding traditional methods (maximum accuracy of 75%). When determining ore element content, the model predicts target element content with an error of less than 5%, achieving a ground truth classification accuracy of 91%, surpassing methods such as SVM (80%) and SOMP (77%). This paper provides technical support for intelligent mining production.
