AB  - Soil moisture (SM) regulates runoff, evaporation, and vegetation with climatic influences. Scientists seek to predict surface SM through regression models, data fusion, and data-driven approaches. However, natural factors affecting SM are intricate and interrelated, especially with changing climates, making estimation difficult with conventional statistical methods. This study aims to analyze climate change effects on SM from 2016 to 2024, investigate environmental interactions, assess predictors, and create SM estimation models. Analysis shows increasing NDVI, Tmin, and evapotranspiration in winter and post-monsoon, while LST consistently decreases. Changes in SM are positive in winter, with significant impacts of Tminacross seasons. Clustered correlation analysis groups explanatory variables, revealing complex effects on SM variability. SM relationships vary by season, with notable shifts between winter and post-monsoon. The factor detection identifies winter and post-monsoon Tmaxas key factors in determining SM variability (44%, 64%), with pre-monsoon LST also playing a significant role (38%). Interaction analysis highlights the importance of Tmaxand post-monsoon rainfall (71%) in predicting SM changes. XGBoost model outperforms DT and ETR models with high accuracy in estimating SM levels across different seasons. XGBoost achieves the highest accuracy in pre-monsoon and post-monsoon for estimating SM heterogeneity, but its performance is moderate (73%) during winter. XGBoost identifies rainfall, Tmin, and Tmaxas crucial seasonal determinants. This study reveals the complex synergistic effects of environmental and climatic factors on SM over the spatio-temporal heterogeneity. The dynamic climatic nature may threaten the ecosystems and economic practices. Findings can aid government agencies in addressing water challenges effectively.
