AB  - Gully erosion is a widespread phenomenon that can lead to significant environmental and economic problems. In this study, we propose an object-oriented approach as a phase to provide input for gully erosion induced by torrential rainfall in the eastern parts of Golestan Province in Iran. Our approach involves the integration of remote sensing data and data mining techniques to analyze the factors contributing to gully erosion and to develop a gully erosion susceptibility map. The analysis relied on field studies and scrutiny of 81 existing gullies among 385 gullies within the region. For this investigation, we utilized QuickBird satellite images from 2010 and 2021, covering both the pre- and post-periods of intense rainfall in 2019. We performed a segmentation operation on the images. Subsequently, in the Python programming environment (Google Colab), we combined 23 contributing factors that influence gully erosion (GECFs) with the segmentation maps and performed modeling using three machine-learning algorithms: random forest (RF), maximum entropy (ME), and support vector machine (SVM). The results indicated the high accuracy of the object-based oriented method in detecting objects at a scale of 40 in the study area. Before March 18, 2019, torrential rainfall and the distance from the river were the most significant indicators, while after this intense and durable event, rainfall and HAND were the most significant. Among the three machine learning algorithms, RF demonstrated the highest accuracy for both the calibration (AUC = 0.95) and validation stages (AUC = 0.94). Therefore, the area of gullies, as determined by the random forest model, showed an increase of 67.95 ha. The largest increase in area was observed in the category transitioning from 0 to 1 (before to after rainfall), with a total area of 9.2327 ha. The RF model identified 7.86 ha of non-gully areas that have been transformed into gullies, highlighting the influence of precipitation and terrain. The model also revealed significant increases in high-risk categories, with 332.94 ha classified as high-risk and 271.44 ha as very high-risk. Unlike the ME and SVM models, RF provides balanced and realistic results, making it the most reliable model for analyzing gully erosion.
