AB  - In recent years, deep learning-based methods have attracted considerable attention in hyperspectral image (HSI) hyperspectral unmixing (HU) tasks due to their robust feature extraction and powerful fitting capabilities. Among them, convolutional neural networks (CNNs) have gained popularity in HU research. However, they still suffer from limitations in spatial feature extraction and effective integration of spatial–spectral information. To address these issues, a hyperspectral image unmixing method based on dual-path convolution and hybrid attention enhancement mechanism (HAEM) is proposed. Before feature extraction, a dual-path convolution module (DPCM) is introduced to capture both local and global information. The spatial–spectral extraction module is then employed to jointly extract spatial and spectral features from the data. Finally, an HAEM is applied to the obtained spatial and spectral features, thereby providing more effective feature representations for subsequent spatial–spectral fusion. Experimental results on three real datasets and one synthetic dataset demonstrate that the proposed method significantly outperforms six others unmixing methods, validating its effectiveness and potential in hyperspectral image unmixing tasks.
