AB  - Earth observation (EO) data and image processing techniques have been applied to geologic exploration since the 1970s. Over time, and more recently with the advent of new sensors and advanced image processing techniques, such as artificial intelligence (AI) algorithms, EO data has become a powerful tool for delineating target exploration deposit types. However, EO data and AI-driven image processing techniques have only more recently been applied to detect several critical raw materials (CRMs). This study presents the main results and findings of three recent European Union (EU) financed projects (ERA-MIN2, H2020 and HORIZON programs), including tasks related to satellite image processing and spectral analysis applied to CRMs. LIGHTS (http://lights.univ-lorraine.fr/) project, an ERA-MIN2 project (between 2018 and 2021), which aimed to develop techniques for easier and faster detection of Lithium (Li)-host minerals combining drone-borne, satellite data and field observations. GREENPEG (https://www.greenpeg.eu/), a H2020 project started in 2020 and ended in 2024, had a dedicated work package for satellite image processing techniques (including AI techniques) for pegmatite identification. S34I (https://www.s34i.eu/), a HORIZON project, started in 2023 and ended in 2025, aimed to research and innovate new data-driven methods to analyze EO data, supporting systematic mineral exploration and continuous monitoring of extraction, closure and post-closure mining activities to increase European autonomy regarding raw materials resources and to use EO data and AI techniques. In this paper, the main objectives of these projects regarding EO data and image processing, the data used, the methods employed/developed, and the main findings will be presented.
