AB  - An integrated weather forecasting framework was developed by combining traditional Vedic Panchangam principles with modern machine learning techniques to enhance long-range climate prediction. In this approach, astronomical indicators such as Tithi, Nakshatra, Paksha, Ritu, Yoga, and Karana were incorporated alongside conventional meteorological variables including temperature, rainfall, wind speed, and rolling climatic averages. These hybrid features were processed through data cleaning, exploratory analysis, and feature engineering before being used to train Random Forest models for both rainfall classification and temperature regression. Seasonal normalization was applied to align Vedic cycles with atmospheric variations, and model performance was evaluated through accuracy metrics, feature importance analysis, and Vedic validation parameters. Strong correlations between lunar-solar transitions and precipitation patterns were identified, and improved prediction stability was achieved through the use of rolling statistical windows. A Streamlit-based application was designed to provide real-time predictions, Vedic interpretations, and explainability outputs, enabling user-friendly interaction with the hybrid system. Through this combined methodology, reliable long-range forecasting was demonstrated, and meaningful contributions from Vedic indicators to seasonal trend prediction were observed. Future enhancements were suggested through the integration of satellite data, advanced deep-learning architectures, and real-time ephemeris computation.
