AB  - Agricultural resilience in the Philippines, particularly in the disaster-prone Region VIII (Eastern Visayas), is hindered by a lack of localized, predictive tools for crop monitoring. This study evaluates the efficacy of three distinct computational architectures-Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA), Space-Time Autoregressive Moving Average (STARMA), and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks-in forecasting the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI). Using the Google Earth Engine (GEE) API, a high-resolution multi-channel data repository was engineered, integrating CHIRPS precipitation and soil classification layers as exogenous drivers. This framework allowed for the precise spatial alignment of environmental variables, enabling a rigorous comparison between univariate temporal models, non-linear deep learning systems, and spatio-temporal frameworks. Results indicate that the STARMA model, utilizing a Gaussian Process regression with a Matérn kernel, outperformed all other architectures, achieving the highest $R^{2}$ of 0.7503. Residual analysis further validated these findings, revealing a strong Pearson correlation $(r=0.864)$ between the model's NDVI forecasts and ground-truth crop yield data from the Philippine Statistics Authority (PSA). By identifying the optimal methodology for capturing the complex, non-linear dynamics of tropical vegetation health, this research contributes a robust, data-driven framework to support strategic agricultural decision-making and climate adaptation in the Philippines.
