AB  - Soil moisture is crucial for understanding the interactions between soil, atmosphere, and hydrological processes. The limited availability of in-situ measurements, and uncertainties in satellite-based and reanalysis datasets pose significant challenges to soil moisture estimation. To enhance the accuracy of soil moisture estimation, this study proposes an optimal selection and fusion framework of multi-source soil moisture data based on climate zoning and cross attention mechanism, which allows to maximize the advantages of each soil moisture product in terms of coverage periods and quality. Specifically, the framework employs a CNN-LSTM architecture for spatiotemporal feature extraction and data fusion, where convolutional neural networks (CNN) initially capture spatial patterns from multi-source soil moisture data, and long short-term memory networks (LSTM) is followed to model their temporal dependencies. Considering different soil moisture products behave differently in different climate zones, the framework establishes several auxiliary data (ERA5-L skin temperature, precipitation and DEM)-guided cross attention weighting sub-modules for multi-source data fusion which can automatically learn the quality advantages of each product. To illustrate the framework, three types of soil moisture products (SMAP, ERA5-L and ESA CCI) and the in-situ data in the Yellow River basin are selected for data fusion. The results demonstrate that soil moisture generated by the proposed framework achieves an unbiased root mean square error (ubRMSE, m3/m3) of 0.043, showing 47%, 35%, and 17% reductions compared to the original SMAP, ERA5-L, and ESA CCI data, respectively. In addition, the proposed framework also shows robustness in climate zoning granularity, feature processing strategies, and spatial representativeness of stations. Finally, an improved soil moisture dataset (daily, 0.1°) ranging from 1950 to 2021 is produced for the Yellow River basin. This study provides a new practical framework for soil moisture estimation improvement, which will benefit basin hydrology and water resources management.
