AB  - Accurate estimation of above-ground biomass (AGB) is critical for carbon accounting, climate mitigation, and forest monitoring. However, estimating AGB in structurally complex and high-biomass forests such as the Hyrcanian forests of Iran remains challenging. The estimation challenge is further exacerbated by saturation effects in remote sensing signals, particularly in old-growth forests where biomass exceeds the sensor’s sensitivity thresholds. In this context, integrating multimodal satellite data with advanced machine learning techniques is crucial to enhance model robustness and capture structural variability more effectively. This study investigates the effectiveness of multisource satellite data, including Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2, and Landsat-8, along with canopy height model (CHM) and topographic variables in predicting AGB across a large forested landscape. Using field data from 2,543 systematically distributed national forest inventory plots, we tested five machine learning algorithms: Random Forest (RF), Support Vector Regression (SVR), k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Linear Regression (LR), and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost). Feature sets integrating canopy height, spectral vegetation indices, and elevation consistently outperformed those based on radar backscatter or texture alone. The highest model performance (R2 = 0.47, RMSE ≈ 79 Mg ha−1) was achieved using the full feature stack. However, all models exhibited saturation effects beyond 200 Mg ha−1, limiting prediction accuracy in dense forest stands. Model interpretation using SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) indicated that canopy height, elevation, and red-edge vegetation indices were the most influential predictors. Our results demonstrate both the potential and limitations of using open-access satellite data and machine learning for large-scale AGB estimation in complex forest ecosystems.
