AB  - Recent advancements in machine learning and deep learning have considerably enhanced remote sensing image classification, expanding beyond traditional convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to include diverse models and feature extraction techniques. This study investigates the effectiveness of both traditional machine learning and modern deep learning methods for semantic segmentation and pixel-wise classification of high-resolution remote sensing imagery, emphasizing land use and land cover (LULC) tasks under data limitation constraints. A systematic survey compares state-of-the-art approaches, including hand-crafted and automatic feature extraction, and analyzes strategies such as transfer learning, data augmentation, and active learning to address data scarcity and model transferability. Through a comparative research analysis, the work highlights practical challenges, including computational requirements, data scarcity, and transferability, that impact model deployment in real-world remote sensing scenarios. Key findings identify persistent limitations, assess the strengths of segmentation and classification frameworks, and propose future research directions for efficient, adaptable, and scalable remote sensing solutions.
