AB  - The pronounced spatial heterogeneity of soil moisture content (SMC) in arid-region apple orchards presents significant challenges for monitoring. Furthermore, current remote sensing models are limited by their poor generalization capabilities across diverse agroecological conditions. To overcome these limitations, this study proposes an SMC prediction framework, STARNet (Spectral-Transformation Attention Residual Network), based on unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) hyperspectral remote sensing and a dual-branch deep learning architecture. The model integrates one-dimensional convolutional neural networks (1D CNN) to extract local correlated features from spectral band sequences and two-dimensional residual networks (2D ResNet) to extract structured features from restructured spectral feature maps. Additionally, a lightweight channel attention mechanism (SE module) is incorporated to enhance the adaptive representation of moisture-sensitive bands. Using multi-date UAV hyperspectral datasets from flat irrigated and mountainous rain-fed apple orchard scenarios in Jingning County, Gansu Province, we employed a local-perturbation data augmentation strategy to improve training stability under limited field samples. Experimental results showed that STARNet maintained stable predictive performance on independent unaugmented field-measured validation sets. In the flat irrigated orchard validation set Sa2, the model achieved R², RMSE, and RPD values of 0.88, 1.52%, and 2.91, respectively; in the mountainous rain-fed orchard validation set Sb2, the corresponding values were 0.86, 1.73%, and 2.67, respectively. These results were superior to those of the best-performing traditional models under the same validation conditions. Ablation experiments indicated that the dual-branch structure and SE module improved the model’s representation of moisture-sensitive spectral information. High-resolution spatial mapping showed that near-surface SMC in the flat irrigated orchard in July had higher values near irrigation facilities, whereas the mountainous rain-fed orchard exhibited more pronounced patchy spatial differentiation. These results revealed differences in near-surface soil moisture spatial patterns across contrasting orchard scenarios. This study reduces the dependence of traditional hyperspectral modeling on manual feature selection and provides high-resolution diagnostic information for near-surface soil moisture monitoring, drought-risk identification, and precision irrigation management support in arid-region apple orchards.
