AB  - This article presents a spatiotemporal litchi tree health classification model using UAV-based multispectral imagery and photogrammetric analysis. The proposed model integrates two key features; vegetation health indices and crown area dynamics, captured over three consecutive harvesting seasons (2022-2024). A crown area estimation module, developed using segmentation and photogrammetry, achieved a high precision with an average absolute error of 0.024 m2. These features were used to train a health classification model employing a multi-layer perceptron (MLP), which classified tree health into healthy, moderately healthy, and unhealthy classes, achieving a mean average precision (mAP) of 95.32%, Recall of 93.57%, and F1-Score of 94.81%. The integration of crown area dynamics significantly improved classification accuracy and revealed strong correlations with vegetation indices. The proposed model offers a robust, non-invasive, and scalable approach for litchi tree health monitoring and classification to supports precision agriculture through improved decision-making and data driven approach.
