AB  - The domain adaptation method for cross-domain classification alleviates the dependence of deep learning on a large amount of labeled data in hyperspectral image classification, but it still requires a relatively adequate number of labeled samples from the source domain to achieve stable transfer effects. Meanwhile, cross-domain few-shot classification is constrained by distribution discrepancies and category mismatches between source and target domains. To address this challenge, this article proposes a dual-stream cross-domain network (DSCD) based on existing few-shot networks, aiming to explore how to effectively transfer source-domain knowledge to improve learning efficiency in the target domain under limited annotations. Within a metalearning paradigm, DSCD integrates feature discriminability and domain adaptability through a two-stage collaborative optimization mechanism to improve cross-domain few-shot classification performance. In the first stage, a spectral-spatial weighting module is designed by combining a spectral-spatial joint attention mechanism with depthwise separable convolutions, which enhances feature discriminability while simplifying the model structure. In the second stage, a feature embedding module (FEM) is developed to leverage the complementary strengths of 2-D and 3-D convolutions. Utilizing octave convolution, FEM facilitates a dual-path interaction between low- and high-frequency components, along with the interfrequency feature exchange mechanism, enabling deep mining of spectral-spatial representations. Experiments on four benchmark datasets - Pavia Center, Pavia University, Salinas, and Indian Pines - demonstrate that DSCD achieves the overall classification accuracies of 97.49%, 82.59%, 92.02%, and 77.89%, respectively. Furthermore, all experiments were conducted under a cross-domain one-shot few-shot setting, where only one labeled sample per category in the target domain was provided for model adaptation. These results indicate that DSCD not only outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods but also exhibits strong generalization and cross-domain adaptability.
