AB  - Cross-domain hyperspectral image classification (HSIC) under few-shot conditions remains a formidable challenge due to severe data scarcity and pronounced cross-domain discrepancies. Recent advances in transductive few-shot learning (TFSL) offer a promising paradigm for tackling this problem. However, effective feature encoding, which is fundamental to TFSL, remains challenging for HSIs due to their complex high-order spectral-spatial correlations. Moreover, most existing TFSL methods assume a uniform class prior over query samples, which introduces severe bias under domain distribution mismatch and class imbalance, leading to significant performance degradation. To this end, we propose a novel method termed TFSL with high-order transformer-few shot learning (HOT-FSL), which mainly consists of three key components: high-order transformer encoder (HOTE), prototype-conditioned gated interaction module (PCGI), and conditional transport-based prototype-oriented unbiased transfer (CTPUT). The proposed HOTE employs factorized high-order attention with Kronecker-structured formulations to jointly model spatial-spectral dependencies and long-range interactions in HSI. PCGI models class prototypes from the support set and adaptively injects them into query features via contrastive routing, thereby enhancing feature discriminability and class consistency. During inference, CTPUT employs conditional transport (CT) with forward-backward navigators and an expectation-maximization (EM)-based solver to iteratively learn unbiased class statistics and refine prototypes under imbalanced query distributions. Comprehensive experimental results on three widely used benchmarks consistently validate the superior performance of the proposed method. The code of this article is available at https://github.com/1546342746/HOT-FSL
