AB  - In recent years, Cross-View Image Matching (CVIM) between aerial Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) imagery and satellite maps is a critical task for autonomous drone navigation. Existing methods often depend on handcrafted similarity metrics and fixed feature fusion strategies, which limit robustness to scale, illumination, and viewpoint variations. This paper proposes a Contrastive Learning-based CVIM Network (CL-CVIMNet) to enhance discriminative feature representation and retrieval accuracy for UAV geo-localization. Initially, data is collected from Visible-Thermal UAV Tracking (VTUAV) dataset includes satellite image pairs sourced from publicly available geo-localization datasets and custom drone flight missions. After that, Preprocessing includes geometric alignment, histogram equalization, and random perspective augmentation to simulate varying viewpoints. Feature extraction is achieved through a dual-stream Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) with cross-attention based feature alignment to process UAV and satellite inputs independently. Then, a contrastive loss function with hard-negative mining drives the embedding space to cluster matching pairs while separating non-matching views. Experimental results demonstrates that CL-CVIMNet achieves retrieval accuracy of 85.58% and mean average precision of 79.24%, when compared with existing Similarity Computation and Feature Fusion (SCFF)-based CVIM model.
