AB  - The use of hyperspectral imagery has emerged as a vital research area in applications ranging from agriculture, medical diagnosis, food quality assessment, monitoring of the environment and mineral exploration among others. This article makes use of hyperspectral imagery and machine learning to devise an innovative approach to distinguishing between gold and non-gold items. Using hyperspectral images captured with a Specim IQ camera, 204 spectral bands across a range of wavelengths (397.32 to 1003.58 nm) are analyzed to identify an optimal subset of 66 bands that minimizes computational costs while maximizing classification performance. Impressive scores of 0.98 Recall, 0.88 F1-score, and an AUC of 0.91 bear testimony to the spectacular performance even with a small-sized dataset. It may be emphasized that the current work is the first of its kind, offering an efficient, cost-cutting solution to the task of determining if an object is indeed made of gold. This work also paves the way for designing a hyperspectral camera, specifically for this purpose and operating with only 66 bands.
