AB  - Amidst rapid advancements in remote sensing technology, characterized by sensor sophistication and increasing data availability, the potential for spatially and temporally extracting detailed vegetation information has significantly expanded. To handle the processing requirements of such vast data streams and enable accurate quantification of vegetation traits in a spatiotemporal context, reliable and scalable retrieval techniques are essential. This review synthesizes state-of-the-art remote sensing retrieval methods for inferring vegetation traits across scales, from leaf to landscape to global levels. The identified retrieval methods are categorized into four groups: (1) parametric regression, i.e., a model that is parametrized—either explicitly by the user or implicitly by the algorithm—according to a predefined mathematical structure; (2) non-parametric regression, also known as machine learning regression algorithms (MLRAs), a data-driven approach where the model structure is not pre-specified but instead statistically learned directly from the data; (3) physically-based approaches involving inversion of radiative transfer models (RTMs); and (4) hybrid regression methods, which combine RTM simulations with MLRAs. Parametric and nonparametric models commonly rely on empirical data, while RTM-based and hybrid approaches primarily employ theoretically derived data simulated through radiative transfer modeling. This chapter systematically discusses each category, reviewing established methods for vegetation trait mapping, incorporating recent advancements, and providing examples of contemporary mapping applications. Latest developments and high-performance methodologies are identified. To achieve operational processing that is transferrable in the temporal and spatial domains, effective retrieval methods should not only be accurate but also provide reliable uncertainty measures and exhibit rapid runtime. An emerging trend in large-scale satellite data stream processing involves integrating hybrid retrieval models into scalable cloud-computing platforms.
ER  -

