AB  - The Asia-Pacific region faces an escalating climate risk landscape marked by frequent floods, tropical cyclones, droughts, wildfires, and sea-level rise. Traditional Earth observation (EO) satellites such as Sentinel-2, Landsat, and MODIS have been invaluable for monitoring these hazards, but inherent limitations in either spatial resolution or revisit frequency constrain timely, high-detail insights. This paper presents the EarthDaily Constellation, a forthcoming commercial satellite system comprising 10 satellites with 22 spectral bands (visible to thermal infrared) at 5 m resolution, as a new paradigm for climate resilience. The EarthDaily Constellation is designed for daily global coverage, delivering analysis-ready data (ARD) with high radiometric quality, low latency (<12 hours), and AI-ready consistency [1][2]. We compare EarthDaily's capabilities with legacy missions (Sentinel-2, Landsat-8/9, MODIS), highlighting improvements in temporal frequency and detail. We then explore hypothetical use cases across agriculture, disaster management, mining, and coastal applications to illustrate how daily 5 m data can transform early warning, impact assessment, and adaptation planning in the Asia-Pacific. A methodology for integrating EarthDaily data with other data sources (e.g., Sentinel, weather data, in-situ sensors) is outlined, leveraging anomaly detection, trend analysis, and predictive modeling techniques on EarthDaily's cloud-free ARD imagery. Proposed results indicate that daily high-resolution monitoring could substantially improve drought early warnings, real-time flood mapping, wildfire detection, infrastructure resilience monitoring, and coastal change assessments relative to current practices. We discuss the broader implications for national adaptation strategies and EO data standards, arguing that EarthDaily's daily, AI-ready imagery offers a critical new resource for sovereign climate resilience efforts in the Asia-Pacific. Finally, we conclude with the role of commercial providers like EarthDaily in complementing public satellite programs, enabling a new era of data-driven climate adaptation and disaster risk reduction.
