AB  - Very high resolution (VHR) satellite images have been on the market for several decades, but their automatic and precise geometric correction remains a major challenge. This paper presents the new version of the geometric processing module of the STORM processing chain. The module is able to automatically orthorectify VHR satellite images using downloadable ancillary data and deep learning methods for road extraction. As a result, processing is smooth and no prior manual retrieval and preparation of spatial data is required. Ground control points (GCPs) are extracted by matching freely available vector roads from web databases with roads extracted from the input satellite image. The procedure consists of different steps, from ancillary data preparation, road detection and ground control points extraction to orthorectification using a lidar digital terrain model. The proposed procedure is able to achieve results with an RMSE of significantly less than one pixel on WorldView-3 images. The use of an advanced ancillary data preparation process and deep learning for GCP extraction is a novel approach for automatic orthorectification chains.
