AB  - Change detection (CD) is essential for various real-world applications, such as urban management and disaster assessment. Numerous CD methods have been proposed, and considerable results have been achieved recently. However, detecting changes in hard regions, for example, the boundaries of changes, small changes, and irrelevant pseudo-changes caused by background clutter, remains difficult for these methods, since they pay equal attention to all regions in bi-temporal images. This article proposes a novel CD network, termed HRMNet, which provides accurate change maps via hard region mining. Specifically, the HRMNet performs hard region mining from two different perspectives: 1) a cross-layer knowledge review module (CKRM) is introduced to identify hard regions embedded in high-level features and distill temporal change information from low-level features to high-level features, thereby enhancing feature representation capabilities and 2) an online hard region estimation (OHRE) branch is constructed to model pixel-wise hard samples, supervised by the error between predicted change maps and the corresponding ground truth during training. Finally, the hard region-aware features extracted from the OHRE branch and multilevel temporal difference features are aggregated into a unified feature representation to improve the accuracy of CD. Experimental results on three benchmark datasets demonstrate the superior performance of the HRMNet in the CD task.
