AB  - With the advent of satellites, we have vastly simplified mineral mapping. Satellites are fitted with special cameras or payloads that capture pictures in extremely specific wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation. These are called spectral bands. Minerals have absorption and reflectance in specific wavelengths of energy and using the data from a combination of bands this technology can be used to identify and map the minerals of the study area. The OLI-1 sensor on Landsat-8 captures images in 12 bands from coastal aerosol to thermal infrared on the spectral scale. Landsat-8 was launched in 2013 and is still considered a workhorse for data for remote sensing images. Landsat-9 is the successor to Landsat-8 and was launched in 2021. The OLI-2 sensor is identical to its predecessor. After acquiring images captured by the Landsat-8 and Landsat-9 satellites over the Singh hum district in Jharkhand, a suite of classification algorithms were applied to the images and the results were compared with a georeferenced mineral map of the region.
