AB  - Satellite image classification has emerged as a focus of interest in computer vision, achieving impressive results due to rapid advancements in Deep Learning (DL). However, DL models often needed a significant amount of time for training; in some cases, training a model may take several days. Moreover, in realworld scenarios, labeled data is often unavailable. In the domain of remote sensing, supervised and semi-supervised techniques have been extensively explored, but self-supervised learning (SSL) holds significant potential to address these challenges. This research proposed a modified self-Distillation with No Labels Vision Transformer (Mo-DINO-ViT) architecture that uses SSL to extract meaningful features without labels. The extracted features are then reduced with a dimensionality reduction technique, i.e., Principal Component Analysis (PCA), and fed into a downstream classification task using our proposed modified Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP). The framework is evaluated against state-of-the-art approaches, achieving a superior accuracy of 97.78% while requiring only 26.25 seconds of training time.
