AB  - Ion adsorption rare earth mining activities have caused serious ecological problems. It is critical to monitor mining activities through precise object detection. However, in the special scenes of mining sites captured by high-resolution remote sensing images, the large amount of complex background information intensifies the semantic confusion among geographic entities. Therefore, the accurate detection of ground objects in mining areas remains a challenging task. This paper proposes an innovative multispectral class-constraint attentional neural network, referred to as MCCANet, which can use multispectral channel information to derive intra-class and inter-class implicit semantic relations. It forms attention with class information constraints for imposing stronger class supervision in order to suppress the background information and highlight the feature differences of the foreground object in complex scenes. Furthermore, a dataset dedicated to rare earth mining areas is constructed to validate the efficiency of the proposed method. The obtained results show that MCCANet has high accuracy in targeting potential objects within mining scenes while significantly mitigating the high false alarm rates resulting from complex backgrounds. Notably, when compared to current popular deep learning models, MCCANet outperforms others by achieving the highest level of detection accuracy.
