AB  - The types of wetland resources in lake areas are diverse and complex, and the differences in spectral characteristics of different features can easily lead to a decrease in recognition and monitoring capabilities when extracting wetland remote sensing image information. This article is based on Landsat remote sensing image data. Through image preprocessing and enhancement, spectral and texture features are extracted. Artificial neural networks, maximum likelihood method, random forest and other algorithms are used to extract and classify wetland information, and the classification accuracy and effectiveness of the three methods are compared. The experimental results show that the random forest method reaches 85.18% overall recognition accuracy and 0.8149 Kappa coefficient, which is the most effective in extracting and classifying the features in the lake area. Further extracting the multivariate features of remote sensing data such as spectral features and texture features, the experimental results show that the overall classification accuracy reaches 91.45% and the Kappa coefficient reaches 0.8927, and the classification accuracy is effectively improved.
